🔷 What is the Function Interface?
Function<T, R> is a functional interface in the java.util.function package.

It represents a function that accepts one argument of type T and returns a result of type R.

It is often used in lambda expressions and method references to represent transformation or mapping logic.

🔹 Functional Interface Declaration:
java
Copy
Edit
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Function<T, R> {
    R apply(T t);
}
T → Input type

R → Return type

apply(T t) → Abstract method that must be implemented (used implicitly when using lambdas or method references)

🔸 Why Use Function Interface?
You use it when you want to:

Transform or map data from one type to another.

Encapsulate logic for reuse.

Use lambda expressions or method references for more concise and functional-style code.

✅ Example 1: Using Lambda with Function
java
Copy
Edit
Function<String, Integer> lengthFunction = s -> s.length();
System.out.println(lengthFunction.apply("Hello"));  // Output: 5
✅ Example 2: Using Method Reference
java
Copy
Edit
Function<String, Integer> lengthFunction = String::length;
System.out.println(lengthFunction.apply("Java"));  // Output: 4
✅ Example 3: Composing Functions
The Function interface provides two default methods:

andThen(Function after) – run another function after this one

compose(Function before) – run another function before this one

java
Copy
Edit
Function<Integer, Integer> multiplyBy2 = x -> x * 2;
Function<Integer, Integer> add3 = x -> x + 3;

Function<Integer, Integer> composed = multiplyBy2.andThen(add3); // (x * 2) + 3
System.out.println(composed.apply(5));  // Output: 13
🔍 When to Use Function Interface?
When you want to process data in a functional way.

Commonly used in:

Stream API (map, flatMap)

Chaining transformations

Configurable logic (e.g., passing transformation logic to methods)